course: dictionary
lesson: 7980
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Reconciliation means making friends again after an argument or fight. You can hear the word «reconcile» inside it, which means to bring people back together. When two friends fall out and then make up, you have reconciliation.
explain: «Canada has apologised for the residential schools and is working towards reconciliation.» The clues are apologised and working towards. Saying sorry is the start, and reconciliation is the long process that comes after. It is something you work towards, not just one moment.
explain: Reconciliation has other meanings too. In maths and money, it means checking that two sets of numbers match, like a bank statement and your own record. Ask yourself: are people falling out, or are numbers being checked? The sentence will tell you which meaning is right.
fact: Reconciliation means making friends again after an argument or fight.
fact: Reconciliation can also mean making two ideas or facts agree when they seemed different.
fact: In the Catholic Church, the sacrament of Reconciliation (confession) is where Catholics believe their sins are forgiven.
fact: «National Reconciliation Week» is a time in Australia when people learn about and celebrate reconciliation with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.
fact: In Canada, reconciliation with First Nations peoples is about healing old wrongs and building respect for each other.
exercise: «After the argument, the two brothers shook hands. It was the start of their reconciliation.» What does reconciliation mean here? | A big family meal | Adding up two lists of numbers | A type of weather | *Making friends again after an argument
exercise: Mum says the bank statement and her record have had reconciliation done. What does it mean? | Writing a letter to the bank | *Checking that two sets of numbers match | Spending all the money at once | Losing the bank card
exercise: «Canada has apologised for the residential schools and is working towards reconciliation.» In this sentence, what did Canada do first? | Had a party | Built a new school | Closed the sea | *Apologised
test: In church, what do Catholics believe happens in the sacrament of Reconciliation? | Choirs sing the loudest | *Sins are forgiven | Candles are lit for the day | Bread is baked
test: Which phrase is closest in meaning to making friends again after a fight? | Count the coins | Ignore everyone in the room | *Patch things up | Start a new fight
exam: Which meaning fits «Her two different answers needed reconciliation»? | Forgetting them | Writing them out twice | Taking them apart | *Making them agree
